{"contents":[{"uri":"montagent://schema/effect/chroma.json","mimeType":"application/json","text":"{
  "type": "object",
  "additionalProperties": false,
  "properties": {
    "color": {
      "allOf": [
        {
          "$ref": "montagent://schema/def/Colour.json"
        }
      ],
      "pattern": "^#[0-9A-F]{6}$",
      "description": "The screen colour, `#RRGGBB`. Not `#RRGGBBAA`: an alpha on the key colour is meaningless and would be a second way to say nothing — a key colour names a colour to find in the frame, never one to composite (ADR-0088, ADR-0014)."
    },
    "tolerance": {
      "type": "number",
      "format": "double",
      "minimum": 0.0,
      "maximum": 1.0
    },
    "softness": {
      "type": "number",
      "format": "double",
      "minimum": 0.0,
      "maximum": 1.0
    },
    "spill": {
      "type": "number",
      "format": "double",
      "minimum": 0.0,
      "maximum": 1.0
    },
    "name": {
      "type": "string",
      "const": "chroma"
    }
  },
  "required": [
    "name",
    "color",
    "tolerance",
    "softness",
    "spill"
  ],
  "description": "Key a screen colour out of the element's pixels (ADR-0088).\n\n**A matte operation, not a colour one.** Its output is transparency: it decides\nwhich pixels survive, and the RGB it keeps is the RGB it was given. That is why\nADR-0049's stopping rule — which opens *\"a **colour operation** is admissible in v1\nonly if…\"* — does not reach the keying, and why `color` is a literal `#RRGGBB`\nrather than `tint.color`'s closed exception being cited a second time.\n\n`color` is the screen colour, in the one spelling ADR-0014 fixes for every colour in\nthe format. Not `#RRGGBBAA`: an alpha on the key colour is meaningless and would be\na second way to say nothing — [`Effect::checked`] refuses one.\n\n`tolerance` is the normalised distance in the chroma plane within which a pixel is\nkeyed out, identity `0` — which keys **nothing**, and makes the whole member a\nno-op. `softness` is the width of the partial-alpha band at the edge of the key,\nidentity `0` — a hard, binary matte. `spill` suppresses screen colour reflected onto\nthe subject, identity `0`; it is the one parameter that changes RGB, and ADR-0088\nadmits it on ADR-0049's four clauses directly rather than by any exception.\n\nAll three are `0.0`–`1.0`, which the derive cannot state and [`Effect::checked`]\ndoes.\n\nStatic, like every effect parameter (ADR-0012). So the member keys a screen that is\nuniform **in time**: footage whose lighting drifts mid-take needs the element cut at\nthe drift boundaries, and `measure`'s per-frame coverage reading is how those\nboundaries are found. That is a stated limitation of ADR-0088, not a gap."
}
"}]}

